    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/bak-hyeokgeose"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bak-hyeokgeose.jpg" alt="Bak Hyeokgeose" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/bak-hyeokgeose">Bak Hyeokgeose</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%EC%B4%88%EB%93%B1%EB%8C%80%ED%95%9C%EC%97%AD%EC%82%AC_%EC%A0%9C2%ED%8E%B8_2.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Original: Jeong Inho Transcription: History Net, National Institute of Korean History, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Silla grew from Saro, one of the twelve statelets of the Jinhan confederacy in south-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a>, centred on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gyeongju">Gyeongju</a> basin. The <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samguk-sagi">Samguk sagi</a></em>, compiled by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kim-busik">Kim Busik</a> in 1145, dates its foundation to 57 BCE, when the six villages of the basin are said to have chosen as ruler <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bak-hyeokgeose">Bak Hyeokgeose</a>, who according to legend hatched from an egg found beside a well. This early chronology, which makes Silla older than its rivals Goguryeo and Baekje, is generally treated as legendary, and Saro was probably a chiefdom of modest size until the third century CE. The early throne passed among three lineages: the Bak, the Seok, beginning with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/talhae-of-silla">Talhae</a>, and the Kim, whose ancestor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kim-alji">Kim Alji</a> was also said to have appeared miraculously. From the reign of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naemul-of-silla">Naemul</a> (r. 356–402), who used the title <em>maripgan</em>, the Kim held the throne by hereditary succession, and Saro gradually absorbed the other Jinhan polities. In 377 and 382 Silla envoys reached the Former <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-dynasty">Qin</a> court in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> in company with Goguryeo missions. Pressed by Wa raiders from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> and by Gaya, Naemul sought help from Goguryeo, and according to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gwanggaeto-stele">Gwanggaeto Stele</a> an army of that king came south in 400 to relieve Silla, which remained under Goguryeo influence for much of the fifth century.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/gyeongju"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gyeongju.jpg" alt="Gyeongju" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/gyeongju">Gyeongju</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gyeongju_montage.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Kyoww (montage), CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Silla society was ordered by the bone-rank system (<em>golpum</em>), which fixed status by birth. At the top stood the 'sacred bone' (<em>seonggol</em>) royal lineage, which died out with Queen <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jindeok-of-silla">Jindeok</a> in 654, and the 'true bone' (<em>jingol</em>) aristocracy; below them came head ranks six to four and commoners. Rank determined the offices a man could hold and even the size of his house, the colours of his clothing and the carriages he could use. The economy rested on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a> and other grains grown by peasants who owed taxes and labour service, and the state granted aristocrats and officials the revenue of villages and, later, land. Silla also had rich gold resources and skilled goldsmiths: royal tombs of the fifth and sixth centuries at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gyeongju">Gyeongju</a>, such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hwangnam-daechong">Hwangnam Daechong</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cheonmachong">Cheonmachong</a>, have yielded gold crowns with tree- and antler-shaped uprights, gold belts and glass vessels from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> world. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beopheung-of-silla">King Beopheung</a> recognised <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a> in 527 after the martyrdom of the courtier <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ichadon">Ichadon</a>, and it became the faith of court and aristocracy. Under Unified Silla, Gyeongju grew into a large city, and trade with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tang-dynasty">Tang China</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> flourished; in the ninth century the merchant-commander <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jang-bogo">Jang Bogo</a> controlled shipping across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yellow-sea">Yellow Sea</a> from his base at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cheonghaejin">Cheonghaejin</a> on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wando">Wando</a>, and Silla communities lived in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shandong">Shandong</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/seong-of-baekje"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/seong-of-baekje.jpg" alt="Seong of Baekje" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/seong-of-baekje">Seong of Baekje</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:GUZE_Kannon_Horyuji.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Tokyo Bijutsu Gakko, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Silla's military rise began in the sixth century. In 512 its general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isabu">Isabu</a> subdued the island state of Usan, modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ulleungdo">Ulleungdo</a>, and in 532 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beopheung-of-silla">King Beopheung</a> annexed Geumgwan Gaya at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gimhae">Gimhae</a>, whose royal family joined the Silla aristocracy. King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jinheung-of-silla">Jinheung</a> (r. 540–576) allied with Baekje to drive Goguryeo from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-river">Han River</a> valley in 551, then seized Baekje's share in 553, gaining access to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yellow-sea">Yellow Sea</a> and direct contact with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>. When Baekje struck back, its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seong-of-baekje">King Seong</a> was killed at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-gwansan-fortress">Battle of Gwansan Fortress</a> in 554, and in 562 Silla conquered Daegaya, the last of the Gaya states. Stelae recording Jinheung's tours of his new lands survive on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bukhansan">Bukhansan</a> and at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/changnyeong-county">Changnyeong</a>, Hwangchoryeong and Maunnyeong. The army combined aristocratic commanders, local levies and the <em>hwarang</em>, bands of young aristocrats trained in arms and moral discipline, who produced leaders such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kim-yu-sin">Kim Yusin</a>. After Baekje took many Silla fortresses in 642, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muyeol-of-silla">Kim Chunchu</a> secured an alliance with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tang-dynasty">Tang China</a> in 648. Silla and Tang forces destroyed Baekje in 660, after Kim Yusin overcame the Baekje general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gyebaek">Gyebaek</a> at Hwangsanbeol, and took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pyongyang">Pyongyang</a> in 668. Silla then fought its former ally from 670, winning at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-maeso-fortress">Maesoseong</a> in 675 and in a naval battle at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-gibeolpo">Gibeolpo</a> in 676.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/jijeung-of-silla"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jijeung-of-silla.jpg" alt="Jijeung of Silla" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/jijeung-of-silla">Jijeung of Silla</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Korea-Gyeongju-Cheonmachong-Entrance-01.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">by Julien Ambrosiano at Flickr, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Silla developed from a federation of six aristocratic districts into a centralised monarchy. Early rulers bore titles such as <em>geoseogan</em>, <em>isageum</em> and <em>maripgan</em>; King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jijeung-of-silla">Jijeung</a> adopted the Chinese title <em>wang</em> and fixed the dynastic name Silla in 503, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beopheung-of-silla">King Beopheung</a> promulgated a code of administrative law in 520, organised a seventeen-grade official hierarchy and in 536 proclaimed Silla's own era name. The council of true-bone nobles, the <em>hwabaek</em>, decided major questions by unanimity and could depose kings. Two queens regnant ruled in the seventh century: Queen <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seondeok-of-silla">Seondeok</a> (r. 632–647), who faced the rebellion of the noble <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bidam">Bidam</a> in her last days, and Queen <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jindeok-of-silla">Jindeok</a> (r. 647–654). The creation of the Chancellery (<em>jipsabu</em>) in 651 strengthened royal control. After unification King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sinmun-of-silla">Sinmun</a> (r. 681–692) purged rebellious nobles, divided the realm into nine provinces (<em>ju</em>) and five secondary capitals, founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gukhak">National Confucian Academy</a> (<em>gukhak</em>) in 682, and in 689 abolished the stipend villages of officials in favour of salaries, though they were restored in 757. The central army was reorganised into nine 'oath bannerman' divisions (<em>seodang</em>) that included former Goguryeo and Baekje subjects and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mohe">Malgal</a> soldiers, binding the conquered peoples to the new state.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/bulguksa"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bulguksa.jpg" alt="Bulguksa" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/bulguksa">Bulguksa</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lotus_Flower_Bridge_and_Seven_Treasure_Bridge_at_Bulguksa_in_Gyeongju,_Korea.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>By uniting the peninsula south of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taedong-river">Taedong River</a> in 676, Silla created the first state to rule the greater part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a>, and its language, the main ancestor of later Korean, its aristocracy and its capital culture shaped all later Korean states. Unified Silla's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a> reached a high level: the monk <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wonhyo">Wonhyo</a> (617–686) wrote influential commentaries and spread devotion among ordinary people, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uisang">Uisang</a> (625–702) founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hwaeom">Korean Hwaeom</a> school, and the pilgrim <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hyecho">Hyecho</a> travelled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> in the 720s and left a record of his journey. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulguksa">Bulguksa</a> temple and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seokguram">Seokguram</a> cave shrine, begun in the mid-eighth century under the minister <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kim-daeseong">Kim Daeseong</a>, are among the masterpieces of East Asian Buddhist art and are <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">World Heritage</a> sites. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/divine-bell-of-king-seongdeok">Divine Bell of King Seongdeok</a>, completed in 771, is one of the largest surviving bells in Korea, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cheomseongdae">Cheomseongdae</a> tower at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gyeongju">Gyeongju</a>, traditionally dated to Queen <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seondeok-of-silla">Seondeok</a>'s reign, is often described as the oldest surviving observatory in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-asia">East Asia</a>. Silla scholars such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/choe-chiwon">Choe Chiwon</a> passed the Tang examinations and wrote in classical Chinese. The <em>idu</em> and <em>hyangchal</em> systems of writing Korean with Chinese characters preserved the <em>hyangga</em>, the earliest Korean poems, some of which survive in Iryeon's <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samguk-yusa">Samguk yusa</a></em>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/choe-chiwon"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/choe-chiwon.jpg" alt="Choe Chiwon" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/choe-chiwon">Choe Chiwon</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Choi_Chi-won.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Silla's decline began after the reign of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hyegong-of-silla">Hyegong</a>, who was killed in 780 during an aristocratic revolt. In the following 155 years twenty kings reigned, many after violent succession disputes among true-bone families, and the rebellion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kim-heonchang">Kim Heonchang</a> in 822, who proclaimed a state called Jangan, showed how far provincial governors could challenge the capital. The bone-rank system excluded talented men of lower rank, including returned Tang graduates such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/choe-chiwon">Choe Chiwon</a>, while local strongmen (<em>hojok</em>) who controlled castles, land and private armies, and maritime <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magnates">magnates</a> such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jang-bogo">Jang Bogo</a>, grew increasingly independent. Under Queen <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jinseong-of-silla">Jinseong</a> (r. 887–897) attempts to collect taxes from exhausted provinces provoked peasant uprisings, beginning with that of Wonjong and Aeno at Sabeolju in 889. The rebel leaders <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gyeon-hwon">Gyeon Hwon</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gung-ye">Gung Ye</a> carved out Later Baekje (from 892, proclaimed in 900) and Later Goguryeo (901), later renamed Taebong, reducing Silla to the area around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gyeongju">Gyeongju</a> in the period known as the Later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/three-kingdoms-period">Three Kingdoms</a>. In 918 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taejo-of-goryeo">Wang Geon</a> overthrew Gung Ye and founded Goryeo. Gyeon Hwon sacked Gyeongju in 927 and forced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gyeongae-of-silla">King Gyeongae</a> to take his own life. In 935 King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gyeongsun-of-silla">Gyeongsun</a> surrendered his kingdom peacefully to Goryeo, which absorbed the Silla aristocracy.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Silla, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a>'s longest-lived kingdom, rose from a chiefdom at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gyeongju">Gyeongju</a> to unite most of the peninsula, first in alliance with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tang-dynasty">Tang China</a> and then against it, and its aristocracy, Buddhist art and language laid foundations on which every later Korean state built.</em></p>

